[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Podman Desktop uses the rootful podman API socket
/var/run/podman/podman.sock, so the podman service has to be running.
Enable and start it:

  # sysrc podman_service_enable=YES
  # service podman_service start

Podman Desktop checks the socket every 5 seconds, the engine switches to
"running" once the service is up, a restart of the application is not needed.

Access as a regular user
------------------------
The socket is only accessible to root by default. To use Podman Desktop as a
regular user, give a dedicated group access to the socket:

  # pw groupadd podman
  # pw groupmod podman -m <user>
  # chgrp podman /var/run/podman/podman.sock
  # chmod 660 /var/run/podman/podman.sock

Log out and back in for the group membership to take effect. The socket is
recreated with the default permissions whenever the podman service restarts,
so repeat chgrp/chmod after each restart (or after a reboot).

Containers on FreeBSD
---------------------
Containers on FreeBSD are jails managed by sysutils/ocijail. See the podman
pkg-message and https://podman.io/docs/installation#installing-on-freebsd-140
for the required pf(4) and ZFS setup.

Limitations
-----------
- Podman machines (VMs) and Lima are not available, Podman Desktop only
  manages the native podman engine.
- The "Install" buttons of the Compose, Kind and kubectl extensions download
  Linux binaries that do not run on FreeBSD. Install these tools from
  packages instead, they are detected from PATH (see pkg search
  podman-compose, docker-compose, kubectl or kind).
- Automatic updates are disabled, Podman Desktop is updated with pkg(8).
EOM
}
]
